				<div id="essay">
					<h2>Playing through pain</h2>
					<p>Brooke&#8217;s passion is fast-pitch softball. About a year ago, she was diagnosed with a connective tissue disorder that affected her joints. The doctor said that keeping active throughout her treatment would be very important, even though arthritis had developed.</p>
					<p class="pullquote">Brooke is now free of arthritis and determined to become the best softball player she can be.</p>
					<p>A motivated Brooke continued to practice and play in her softball games throughout the fall season despite her aching joints, which made it difficult for her to run. As the months passed, her arthritis disappeared and the doctors began weaning her off of her medication. Even though she continues to take medication and see a doctor for her condition, she is now free from any symptoms and more determined to become the best softball player she can be.</p>
					<p>When she is not practicing with her team, Brooke finds other ways to improve her skills. She practices hitting and catching every day, either at a local batting cage or at home, where her dad set up a net in the garage. Brooke attends hitting and catching clinics on the weekends.</p>
					<p>Brooke maintains an amazing attitude both on and off of the field, which also encourages her teammates. She continually smiles and cheers for the rest of the team when they are batting. She understands that a positive attitude helps her&#8212;and the rest of the team. Anytime someone asks Brooke to practice with them, she is happy to help, and she thoroughly enjoys spending extra time working with her teammates.</p>
					<p class="last">Brooke understands that practice and diligence are key to achieving her goals. Her ultimate dream is to play college softball, but her current goals are to learn and play catcher, second base, and center field. She likes the challenges at each of these positions, but doesn&#8217;t feel as though she has to be perfect at everything.</p>
				</div>
